    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1132">

                <pb facs="1032" n="1020" xml:id="pg1020"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">760d.61/584: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Finland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Schoenfeld</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Helsinki</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-12-03T11:00:00+02:00">December 3,
                            1939—11 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 3—8:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>401. It having been announced in the press that the Finnish Government has
                    addressed a communication<anchor n="12" xml:id="d1132-f1"/> to the
                        Secretary-General<anchor n="13" xml:id="d1132-f2"/> of the League of Nations
                    invoking the provisions of the Covenant with reference to the aggression against
                    Finland, Minister Swormstadt informed me this morning that though the Finnish
                    Government had little hope of any practicable result from the appeal to the
                        League,<anchor n="14" xml:id="d1132-f3"/> it had been advised by other
                    governments which he did not name to take this step.</p>
                <p>In strict confidence he informed me that yesterday the Swedish Government had
                    been requested to make known to the Soviet Union the willingness of the Finnish
                    Government to negotiate with the Soviet Government regarding its demands and
                    though he did not say so, I gained the impression that the frame of reference
                    for such negotiations would be wider than had thus far been deemed admissible by
                    the Finnish Government.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Schoenfeld</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1132-f1" n="12" xml:id="d1132-f1-content">
                <p>The text of the Finnish appeal to the Council and Assembly of the League of
                    Nations on December 3, 1939, is printed in League of Nations, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Official Journal</hi>, Nos. 11–12 (pt. <hi
                        rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>), November–December 1939, p. 509.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1132-f2" n="13" xml:id="d1132-f2-content">
                <p>Joseph Avenol, of France.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1132-f3" n="14" xml:id="d1132-f3-content">
                <p>For the action initiated by the Secretary General of the League of Nations, see
                    telegram No. 302, December 3, 1939, from the Consul General at Geneva, <ref
                        target="frus1933-39#pg_800">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, The Soviet Union, 1933–1939,
                        p. 800</ref>. For the steps taken in the United States inaugurating what
                    came to be known as the “moral embargo”, see telegram No. 265, December 4, 1939,
                    to the Ambassador in the Soviet Union, and footnote 2g, <ref
                        target="frus1933-39#pg_801">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">ibid</hi>., p. 801</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
